  "question": {
    "text": "The function $f(x)=\\tan ^{-1}(\\sin x+\\cos x)$ is an increasing function in",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "hsuZj",
      "text": "$\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "NiXHg",
      "text": "$\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "7rXGP",
      "text": "$\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "iYXMK",
      "text": "$\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Since, $f(x)=\\tan ^{-1}(\\sin x+\\cos x)$<br />$\\therefore \\mathrm{f}^{\\prime}(\\mathrm{x})=\\frac{1}{1+(\\sin \\mathrm{x}+\\cos \\mathrm{x})^2}(\\cos \\mathrm{x}-\\sin \\mathrm{x})$<br />$=\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\cos \\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)}{1+(\\sin x+\\cos x)^2}$<br />$f(x)$ is increasing if $f^{\\prime}(x)>0 \\Rightarrow \\cos \\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)>0$<br />$\\Rightarrow \\quad-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x+\\frac{\\pi}{4} < \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\Rightarrow-\\frac{3 \\pi}{2} < x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}$",
    "image": null
  },
